.TH p11cert 1 "2024" "smartcard-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
.B p11cert
\- generate certificates from PKCS#11 smartcard keys

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B p11cert.sh
[\fIpkcs11-url\fR]
.br
.B p11cert.sh
.BR \-h | \-\-help

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B p11cert.sh
generates a short-lived self-signed certificate from a PKCS#11 private key
stored on a smartcard or hardware security module. The certificate is appended
to the user's certificate bundle at
.IR ~/.eid/authorized_certificates .

The generated certificate can be used for authentication via
.BR pam_p11 (8),
enabling smartcard-based login with PIN-based key access.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help message and exit.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IR pkcs11-url
Optional explicit PKCS#11 URL to the private key to use. If not provided,
.B p11cert.sh
will attempt to automatically discover and select an appropriate key from
inserted tokens.

The URL must begin with
.BR pkcs11: .

.SH BEHAVIOR

If no PKCS#11 URL is explicitly provided:
.IP \(bu 3
The script queries the system for available PKCS#11 tokens via
.BR p11tool (1).
.IP \(bu 3
It attempts to select an encryption or authentication key first.
.IP \(bu 3
System trust tokens (p11-kit-trust) are automatically filtered out.
.IP \(bu 3
User will be prompted for PIN as needed to access the key.

The output directory
.IR ~/.eid
is created automatically if it does not exist, with permissions
.BR 0755 .

The certificate bundle is updated atomically using a temporary file to prevent
corruption in case of interruption.

If a certificate for the same PKCS#11 public key already exists in the bundle,
it will not be appended again. The script detects duplicates by computing
SHA-256 fingerprints of the public keys.

.SH EXAMPLES

.B Generate a certificate from the default/auto-discovered smartcard key:
.RS
.EX
$ p11cert.sh
.EE
.RE

.B Generate a certificate from an explicit PKCS#11 URL:
.RS
.EX
$ p11cert.sh 'pkcs11:token=MyCard;id=%01'
.EE
.RE

.B List available PKCS#11 keys for manual URL selection:
.RS
.EX
$ p11tool --list-all-privkeys --login --detailed-url --only-urls
.EE
.RE

.SH REQUIREMENTS

The following commands must be available on the system:
.IP \(bu 3
.BR p11tool (1)
\- GnuTLS utility for working with PKCS#11 tokens
.IP \(bu 3
.BR openssl (1)
\- OpenSSL command-line tool with PKCS#11 support

The OpenSSL installation must support PKCS#11 via either:
.IP \(bu 3
.B OpenSSL PKCS#11 provider
(modern, recommended)
.IP \(bu 3
.B Legacy OpenSSL pkcs11 engine
(deprecated but still supported for compatibility)

.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ~/.eid/authorized_certificates
User's certificate bundle. Certificates are appended in PEM format.

.TP
.IR ~/.config/smartcard-tools/ppa.env
Optional per-user configuration file for build-related settings (not used by
.B p11cert.sh
runtime).

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. Certificate was generated and added to the bundle, or already existed.

.TP
.B 1
A required command (p11tool or openssl) is not available, or an unrecoverable
error occurred.

.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments or usage error.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam_p11 (8),
.BR p11tool (1),
.BR openssl (1),
.BR pam-auth-update (8)

.SH NOTES

PIN prompts may appear multiple times:
.IP \(bu 3
Once during key discovery
.IP \(bu 3
Again when checking for duplicate certificates
.IP \(bu 3
A third time when generating the certificate

This is expected behavior and is required to access the smartcard's private key
for these different operations.

To avoid re-entering your PIN, consider using a smartcard reader with PIN caching,
or running multiple operations in sequence.

.SH AUTHOR
Maintained by the smartcard-tools project.
